Industry-standard authorization protocol used to authenticate the AAP-to-SharePoint Graph API connection without exposing user credentials


Service Accounts Requirements
SharePoint
Read-only Graph API application permission scoped to the specific SharePoint site/list (Sites.Selected or equivalent) — not tenant-wide read access
Non-interactive app registration; Client ID/Client Secret stored in AAP's credential vault, never inline in playbooks
Secret rotation policy defined and owner assigned for periodic access review
Ansible
Service account scoped to only the CS-side systems/actions the resulting automation is authorized to touch
Non-interactive, MFA-exempt with compensating controls (IP allow-list, credential vaulting, audit logging)
Execution and decision logic auditable — which ERT row triggered which automation action, with timestamp and outcome
Known Risk Factors
Incorrect or stale data
Ansible acts on ERT data written into memory at retrieval time; a delayed sync, partial pull, or Graph API failure mid-retrieval could cause automation decisions to be based on incomplete or outdated table state
Mitigation: validate record count/checksum before acting; fail closed (no action) on incomplete retrieval
Credential exposure
Client ID/Client Secret must be held in AAP's credential store, not embedded in playbooks or logged in plaintext
Mitigation: AAP Vault credential type; secret rotation cadence; restrict who can view/export the credential object
Overprivileged access scope
A tenant-wide or site-collection-wide Graph API grant would expose more SharePoint content than the ERT list itself
Mitigation: scope the Azure AD app registration to the specific site/list only
Uncontrolled trigger source
If the Archer “true” field can be set by users without validation or approval workflow, it becomes a de facto trigger for automation against CS infrastructure
Mitigation: confirm Archer-side approval/workflow controls on who can set the field, and add a secondary validation check in Ansible before acting
Insufficient audit trail
Without logging of which ERT record drove which automation decision, compliance traceability and incident investigation are impaired
Mitigation: log source record ID, retrieved field values, decision outcome, and execution result for every run
